Article 4
Every female victim of violence has the right to:
a. obtain protection;
b. determine and make her own decisions;
c. obtain information;
d. obtain complaint-handling services;
e. obtain assistance;
f. obtain continuous services up to the rehabilitation stage; and
g. obtain confidential services, whether from individuals, groups, or institutions, whether governmental or non-governmental.

Article 16
Forms of services for the protection of women and women victims of violence include:
a. complaint, consultation and counseling services;
b. assistance services;
c. health services;
d. social rehabilitation services;
e. legal services and/or legal aid; and
f. repatriation and social reintegration services.

Article 14
(1) Prevention of the occurrence of acts of violence against women as referred to in Article 13, shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus that oversee affairs of:
a. health;
b. education;
c. manpower;
d. socio-political;
e. protection and empowerment of women; and
f. peace and order;
(2) Prevention of acts of violence by Regional Apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on the Regional Action Plan with the Regional Apparatus that oversees Protection and Empowerment of Women.

Article 2
(1) Women have the right:
a. to life;
b. to form a family and continue the lineage;
c. to self-development;
d. to obtain justice;
e. to personal freedom;
f. to a sense of security;
g. to welfare; and
h. to participate in government.
(2) Every woman has the right:
a. to live peacefully, safely, in peace, happily, prosperously both physically and spiritually;
b. to be free from all threats of violence and/or exploitation;
c. to live in a family within the bonds of a legal marriage;
d. to sustain life and improve her standard of living;
e. to have work that she likes and is also entitled to fair employment conditions;
f. to obtain education and teaching in all types, levels and pathways of education in accordance with the requirements determined;
g. to a healthy and clean environment;
h. to receive fair wages in accordance with her work that is comparable and commensurate with human dignity;
i. to elect and/or be elected in general elections, regional head elections, village head elections and/or other political position elections based on equal rights through direct, general, free, secret, honest and fair voting according to the applicable laws and regulations;
j. to obtain special protection in the implementation of work or profession against matters that may threaten safety and/or health related to women's reproductive function; and
k. special rights inherent to women due to their reproductive function are guaranteed and protected by law.
l. to obtain legal protection and assistance for problems faced in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
m. other rights in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
(3) Every woman in conditions of disability, old age, pregnancy has the right to obtain facilities and special treatment.
(4) Every elderly woman, with physical and/or mental disability, has the right to obtain care, education, training and special assistance from the Regional Government to ensure a decent life in accordance with human dignity.
(5) The Regional Government shall endeavor to establish an old age protection guarantee system for elderly women.

Article 18
(1) Assistance services as referred to in Article 16 letter b include:
a. assistance to victims during the examination process and health recovery;
b. assistance to victims during the medical examination process for legal purposes;
c. assistance to victims during the examination process at the police, prosecutor's office and court;
d. monitoring the interests and rights of victims in the examination process at the police, prosecutor's office and court;
e. maintaining the privacy and confidentiality of victims from all interested parties, including reporting by mass media;
f. coordinating with other assistants; and
g. providing rehabilitation.
(2) Assistance Services as referred to in paragraph (1) are services, continuous handling up to the stage referred to, consisting of activities and actions carried out by assistants originating from individuals or representatives of institutions that have expertise in providing assistance to victims to conduct counseling, therapy, and advocacy for the strengthening and self-recovery of victims of violence.

